10.00 632.90 Td
(A 1:2 step up transformer. ) Tj
T* ( ) Tj
T* (The transformer is simply two ordinary inductors coupled together using the 'K' ) Tj
T* (coupling statement. ) Tj
T* ( ) Tj
T* (The syntax for this statement is as shown in the schematic where the K defines the statement, ) Tj
T* (the list of the inductors, La, Lb, Lc, etc., define the inductors to be coupled and the number at the ) Tj
T* (end is the coupling coefficient or coupling factor. This defines how closely coupled the inductors are to each ) Tj
T* (other. ) Tj
T* ( ) Tj
T* (A value of 1 defines perfect coupling whilst 0 defines zero coupling between them. ) Tj
T* ( ) Tj
T* (In practice values of between about 0.9 and 0.99 are achievable in real transformers. Lower ) Tj
T* (values may be used for wideband tuned filters built using coupled inductors. ) Tj
T* ( ) Tj
T* (The dotted inductors are used to clearly show the phasing of the windings. The dotted ) Tj
T* (ends are in phase. ) Tj
T* (  ) Tj
T* (Note that a negative coupling coefficient value can be used but it inverts the phase ) Tj
T* (relationship between the primary and secondary windings. It is recommended not to do ) Tj
T* (this as it makes interpreting the phase dots difficult and can cause serious problems ) Tj
T* (in multiwinding coupled inductor circuits.) Tj
T* ( ) Tj
T* (Notice that a small resistance must be included between the voltage source and the ) Tj
T* (primary inductance to stop spice throwing an error about a loop of voltage sources or a ) Tj
T* (voltage source and an inductor. Basically it is not allowed to place any combination of ) Tj
T* (voltage sources or inductors in parallel with each other without some finite resistance ) Tj
T* (between them.) Tj
